! transmit_multiword.strap -- TRANSMIT moving more than one word.
! Reference Manual printed p.30 (PDF p.33), "Data Transmission",
! which is the whole specification in one page.
!
! WHY THIS DECK EXISTS.  StretchExecute's Transmit arm used to execute
! exactly ONE shape -- a one-word IMMEDIATE-count forward transmit --
! and set Operation Code Invalid on every other, "conservative by
! construction" in its own comment.  MCP printed p.4 line 28 is
!
!     X9 A 5   T,11,X BUFER($X1),X BS END      -MOVE MCP IPL
!
! an eleven-word DIRECT-count transmit, so MCP's IPL was never moved.
! The gap was invisible for weeks because the boot test read the
! indicator register AFTER an interrupt had reset it; giving the model
! a disk removed the spurious interrupt and the OP bit appeared.
! docs/NEXT.md "THE NEXT MCP BLOCKER IS TRANSMIT" has the story.
!
! WHAT EACH STEP PINS, all five from printed p.30:
!
!   1. A DIRECT COUNT.  "In direct count operations, the field J, bits
!      51-54, specifies an index register.  The count field, bits
!      28-45, of the index word in this register is used as a full
!      word count."  Step 1 loads index 2 with a count of four and
!      moves four words.  THIS IS MCP'S OWN SHAPE.
!
!   2. AN IMMEDIATE COUNT.  "In immediate count operations, the full
!      word count is specified by instruction bits 51-54."  Step 2
!      moves three words with TI.
!
!   3. A DIRECT COUNT OF ONE, which the old arm refused even though it
!      would have executed it correctly -- its own comment says so.
!
!   4. ASCENDING, ONE WORD AT A TIME.  "In forward transmission, the
!      sending and receiving addresses of each successive word
!      transmitted are obtained by incrementing the previous addresses
!      by one", and the programming note: "if the receiving area
!      overlaps the lower end of the sending area, forward
!      transmission must be used."  Step 4 shifts a block DOWN by one,
!      which only comes out right if the words move in ascending
!      order -- a descending implementation smears the last word over
!      the block, and a snapshot-then-store implementation would need
!      four words of hidden state this machine does not have.
!
!   5. AN IMMEDIATE COUNT OF ZERO IS SIXTEEN.  "A zero count field
!      indicates the maximum count of 16 full words."  A model reading
!      zero as zero moves nothing; one reading it as 2^18 runs off the
!      end of this program.
!
! NOT TESTED HERE, deliberately: SWAP, which still sets Operation
! Code Invalid.  Its two-way interchange is a different datapath and
! >>> "NOTHING IN THE CORPUS EXECUTES ONE" DIED ON 2026-10-23.  <<<
! printed p.40 lines 24 and 28 are "SWAPI,3,$11,Y UX SAV", three
! words from the UNCODE subroutine's normal exit, and line 33 is the
! matched "TI,3,Y UX SAV,$11" with A0 where the SWAPs carry E0.  MCP
! saves three index registers with a SWAP and restores them with
! another, which is what a two-way interchange is for.
! The claim below is kept as written because the history is the
! point of this file, the same way the backward paragraph above is.
!
! nothing in the corpus executes one; its ENCODING is already pinned
! by the jb_ax4b routine 1246 transcriptions
! (decks/ax4b_1246_transmit_swap.strap).
!
! >>> THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "backward transmission and SWAP ...
! MCP NEEDS NEITHER", AND THAT WAS WRONG ABOUT BACKWARD.  MCP printed
! p.19 line 41 is "TB,3,0.0($11),0.0($13)  -MOVE BIASED CTS", and
! with the IOCD card deck on the tape the boot run reaches it: the
! refusal set Operation Code Invalid, the channel status table was
! never relocated, and printed p.20 halted at "XERR 20  BD,$  -YES
! *ERROR* NO CONSOLE" for a machine whose console HAD been declared.
! Steps 6 and 7 are backward, and they are here because a page of
! MCP needed them. <<<
!
!   6. BACKWARD IS A SIGN, NOT A SECOND ALGORITHM.  Reference Manual
!      printed p.30: "In backward transmission, the sending and
!      receiving addresses of each successive word transmitted are
!      obtained by DECREMENTING the previous addresses by one.  The
!      starting addresses specified in the instruction are therefore
!      the addresses of the LAST word in each of the two storage
!      areas."
!
!   7. AND THE SAME PAGE SAYS WHAT IT IS FOR: "When the receiving
!      area overlaps the UPPER end of the sending area, backward
!      transmission must be used; if the receiving area overlaps the
!      lower end of the sending area, forward transmission must be
!      used."  Step 6 is exactly that overlap -- a block shifted UP
!      by one -- which a forward implementation smears, in the mirror
!      of what step 4 does to a descending one.

START   LVI, $X1, 0        !no displacement; the index is here so step
                            !1 has the same (addr)(index) shape MCP's
                            !own transmit does
        LCI, $X2, 4        !index 2's COUNT field = 4 -- a direct count
        T,   2, SRCA($X1), DSTA    !step 1: four words, direct count
        TI,  3, SRCB, DSTB         !step 2: three words, immediate
        LCI, $X3, 1
        T,   3, SRCC, DSTC         !step 3: ONE word, direct count
        LCI, $X4, 4
        T,   4, OVL1, OVL0         !step 4: shift the block down by one
        TI,  0, SRCE, DSTE         !step 5: zero means SIXTEEN
        LCI, $X5, 4
        TB,  5, OVB3, OVB4         !step 6: shift the block UP by one,
                                    !backward, with the receiving area
                                    !overlapping the sender's TOP
        TBI, 3, BWS2, BWD2         !step 7: three words, backward,
                                    !immediate count, NOT overlapping
        BEW, $
        CNOP               !pad to a word boundary -- DD applies no
                            !automatic alignment and the run above is
                            !not a whole number of words.
                            !
                            !IT WAS "NOP, 0" UNTIL 2026-10-22, which
                            !pads by exactly one half-word and is
                            !therefore right only while the run above
                            !has odd parity.  Adding steps 6 and 7 --
                            !two full words and a half -- flipped it,
                            !and every DD below came out half a word
                            !low: the first check read 0x1800A0000000
                            !where it wanted 0xA000000000000001, which
                            !is two adjacent words seen through a
                            !32-bit shift.  CNOP rounds to the next
                            !full word whatever the parity is.

SRCA    DD(BU,64), (16)A000000000000001
        DD(BU,64), (16)A000000000000002
        DD(BU,64), (16)A000000000000003
        DD(BU,64), (16)A000000000000004
DSTA    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

SRCB    DD(BU,64), (16)B000000000000001
        DD(BU,64), (16)B000000000000002
        DD(BU,64), (16)B000000000000003
DSTB    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

SRCC    DD(BU,64), (16)C000000000000001
DSTC    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

! OVL0..OVL4: five words.  Step 4 moves four of them, from OVL1 into
! OVL0, so afterwards OVL0-OVL3 hold D1-D4 and OVL4 is untouched.
OVL0    DD(BU,64), (16)D000000000000000
OVL1    DD(BU,64), (16)D000000000000001
        DD(BU,64), (16)D000000000000002
        DD(BU,64), (16)D000000000000003
        DD(BU,64), (16)D000000000000004

! OVB0..OVB4: five words.  Step 6 moves four of them BACKWARD, from
! OVB3 into OVB4 and down, so afterwards OVB1-OVB4 hold B0-B3 and
! OVB0 is untouched.  THE STARTING ADDRESSES ARE THE LAST WORDS of
! each area, which is what printed p.30 says they are for a backward
! transmit -- OVB3 is the last word of the sending area OVB0..OVB3
! and OVB4 the last of the receiving area OVB1..OVB4.
!
! A FORWARD IMPLEMENTATION OF THIS INSTRUCTION WOULD SMEAR: starting
! at OVB3 and ascending it would copy OVB3 into OVB4, then OVB4 into
! the word above, and OVB1 and OVB2 would never be written at all.
OVB0    DD(BU,64), (16)1000000000000000
OVB1    DD(BU,64), (16)1000000000000001
OVB2    DD(BU,64), (16)1000000000000002
OVB3    DD(BU,64), (16)1000000000000003
OVB4    DD(BU,64), (16)1000000000000004

! Step 7's areas do not overlap, so the only thing it can measure is
! the DIRECTION: a forward run from BWS2 would write BWD2, BWD3 and
! BWD4 and leave BWD0 and BWD1 at F.
BWS0    DD(BU,64), (16)2000000000000000
BWS1    DD(BU,64), (16)2000000000000001
BWS2    DD(BU,64), (16)2000000000000002
BWD0    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
BWD1    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
BWD2    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
BWD3    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
BWD4    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

SRCE    DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000001
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000002
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000003
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000004
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000005
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000006
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000007
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000008
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000009
        DD(BU,64), (16)E00000000000000A
        DD(BU,64), (16)E00000000000000B
        DD(BU,64), (16)E00000000000000C
        DD(BU,64), (16)E00000000000000D
        DD(BU,64), (16)E00000000000000E
        DD(BU,64), (16)E00000000000000F
        DD(BU,64), (16)E000000000000010
DSTE    DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
DSTEND  DD(BU,64), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        END, START
